:: Instant spark {"position":"712.5,1125","size":"100,100"}
''You: ''Love at first sight. Like a spark between us that goes deeper than raw physical lust. A total enmeshment with their entire self. An appreciation and respect of all of their being and a near-instantaneous yet total devotion.

''Me: ''Okay, that sounds like a 'you' thing, or an 'avid reader of YA and Nicholas Sparks novels' thing. Or like you get your life advice from rom coms. Which are--and this is important--works of //fiction//. In many cases, they are no less fantastical and escapist than Lord of the Rings. In other cases, moreso.

But let's entertain the idea that what you are perceiving, regardless of how healthy or stable it may be, something vaguely approaching reality:

That instant connection feeling //could// be a synastry thing.
If you've been Bing searching "Are Scorpios attracted to Sagittarians?" in Edge browser, and were disappointed by ~35% of sites that made it sound vaguely like 'no', let's step back and take heart.

1) Any site that would answer that kind of question at face value is not a site you should be taking seriously and more than anything to do with astrology, at this moment, it would likely behoove you to do some research into techniques for critical thinking. I'm saying this literally, not in a condescending way. Frankly, it seems like a majority of people view the world very credulously, and most public schools (at least in the U.S., if that's where you're from) don't place any great emphasis on those essential skills of media literacy and critical thought. Yet academics from often privileged backgrounds and people with a natural aptitude for skepticism immediately scorn others who lack this skill set as though it was not both culturally normalized and a hugely widespread issue. I'm not doing that. I'm saying, most of us have been there, especially from certain socioeconomic backgrounds which inculcate us systemically into some level of praised/tolerated credulity. It's cool you downloaded my app & I love the curiosity, and maybe you did so out of a newfound skeptical approach toward reality, or maybe it was completely accidental.

In either case, I appreciate you being here and cherish your presence, but! let's focus on understanding how fallacies work and questioning your sources as paramount toward a better engagement with reality and other people. That trusting nature can be a huge impediment in relational stability/success. It may even support an excess of idealism that makes more realistic/practical partnerships seem somehow overly mundane or flawed in ways that are largely illusory, or make poor partnerships feel destined by some external governing factor other than your own instincts and needs. When in fact, one must always come back down to earth and look at the facts, direct observations, and see what actually measures up in the cold light of day, and why people might be telling you the things they are aside from the drive to provide a generous outpouring/exchange of unadulterated truth for the love of Truth itself.

2) Even within the framework of astrology, there's [[way more to compatibility than Sun signs->chart configurations]].

3) Even if astrology is fundamentally valid/useful, [[there's definitely way more to compatibility than astrology.->Theories]]. 
Having created this app and analyzed a LOT of data, I can now say that with certainty. I will concede that there appear to be patterns of relative compatibility/attraction, yes, but they are still not fully deterministic, and moreover: 'compatibility', 'instant attraction', and 'longevity of a relationship' are all separate (albeit loosely related) factors.


:: Interpersonal Context {"position":"1225,1000","size":"100,100"}
No person or couple is completely an island. Most of us, like it or not, are at least somewhat impacted by their social network--family, friends, employers, coworkers, peers. 

That's the microcosm we live in, the broth we're stewing in.
That's before we even get into broader cultural coding and societal expectations, aka the kitchen we're being cooked in.

So yeah, maybe a puppy has a certain innate nature (natal chart), but who's taking care of that puppy (natal charts of relational network)? How is the puppy being raised/trained/fed...or not? That's going to bring out different traits that were possibly always latent but may be otherwise suppressed, and create patterns of how the puppy relates to dogs, cats, humans and other animals; to strangers and friends.

So trying to pet or walk that dog is almost certainly going to be a different experience, even if you raised two dog clones with identical synastric relationships to others.

That's the hypothesis, anyway.

