:: Mundane factors {"position":"1225,1125","size":"100,100"}
''Whaaa? Mundane factors??!''

Yeah, like socioeconomic stuff, and your baseline culture.

One thing I found while using this app to analyze data is that cultural coding is a MAJOR reskinning of any given natal chart.

Here's an example:
There are 6 people. Of the 6, the delineation is along these lines:
* Two grew up in a small town in Georgia.
* Three are hispanic and immersed in Sonoran culture.
* One is hispanic but grew up in SoCal coastal suburbs, surfing with a bunch of prep school kids, and is culturally not just Americanized, but SoCal surfer-coded more than connected to her indigenous ancestry.
* One went to a British public school, then Oxford, and is the child of 2 generations of Tories, who went on to teach at MIT and give a Ted talk.

Of this set, 3 spend most of their lives as musicians, 1 becomes a best-selling novelist, 1 becomes a venture capitalist, 1 wanted to be a full time musician but was pressured to become an attorney instead, and is now the DA because of social pressure & famillial expectation.

Their genders are a mix.

''Here's what I've found from that kind of lineup:''
If three of the charts share Sun, Moon and Mars sign, and the same gender, the two Sonoran musicians are going to resemble each other MUCH more observably from the outside than the third chart who is the Oxford-graduate attorney, but all 3 of them at one point were dead set on being a musician, regardless..

If the third culturally Sonoran chart, is more venture capitalist banker-coded chart, bears less resemblance to the other two Sonoran charts than the Oxford-attorney does. And if you talked to them for awhile, you'd see the Sonoran cultural overlap's impact on all three Sonorans, and the Oxford influence on the Tory progeny, but basic worldview would be strangely aligned between the 2 Sonorans and the attorney, even within their respective frameworks. This isn't about wrapping hotdogs in bacon and a love of polka and folklorico, it's more about shared interpretation from within their cultural lens of things like how masculine self-presentation is or isn't a challenge within certain social frameworks. Meanwhile, the venture capitalist Sonoran is not thinking about this at all; the Sonoran financier has given almost no thought to gender relations, and almost all thought to tangible resource management.

So yes, if you find two people form the same place with similar charts, they wil inevitably have much more common ground for comparison at the onset than two people with similar charts from wildly different backgrounds.

I've also found that some chart configurations appear to be more sensitive to cultural impact and gender coding than others. In some cases, gender matching of similar charts is extremely critical to whether or not two people resemble each other outwardly, whereas in other chart configurations, gender doesn't seem to have much of an impact at all; i.e. some chart personalities/personas/self-presentation is way less intrinsically gender- and culturally-coded than others. 

It's highly speculative at this point and I've barely started testing hypotheses as to why, but it seems as though cultural-malleability is highly variable between [[chart configurations]]. 
Maybe certain configurations support a high degree of persona-curation. That could be motivated by a sincere simple innate people-pleasing tendency, or existing in especially punitive environments where one's default nature must be more repressed, It could, in other cases, indicate Machiavellian power-seeking, or any number of other factors.

[[🔙->Theories]]


:: Natal Charts in Synastry {"position":"1100,1125","size":"100,100"}
''What is a natal chart?''
Broadly speaking, a natal chart is a snapshot of the cosmos as perceived from a geocentric perspective at the time and place a person/entity is born, hatched  or created.

''What is a natal chart measuring anyway? What are we supposed to be deriving from the snapshot of the moment in time and place wherein someone was born?''
Depending on who you ask, the answers differ, including any number of the following:

* basic personality
* aptitudes/skills
* life destiny including: health, finance, partnerships, travel, etc.
* attraction/romance/compatibility
* physical appearance
* vocal quality & other physiogynomy

Most vaguely-respectable astrologers steer clear of the following claims:
* that Sun sign dictates anything in any concrete way
* that one's fate is etched in stone from the moment one is born

''Why Individual Natal Charts Must Be Considered in Synastry Evaluations:''

If, as a thought experiment, we entertain the idea that astrology has any validity, the standard lore has it that each complete natal chart tells a story of who the person is, at their core, on some level. 

Therefore, these natal charts imply a manner in which the individual charts might //respond// to the synastric interchange. 

''Example:'' Chart A is one who can typically be regarded as placid and solemn in most cases. If so, Chart A might not mind the fact that the dynamic between his chart and Chart B's has an inherently serious, fixed character. 

Meanwhile Chart B is a small town waitress with dreams of running away to be a movie star, and Chart A is bumming her tf out with all this 'dadittude', and the fact that their connection is stabilizing and severe makes her feel held back, desperate. 

In other words, whether an aspect is "good" or not is largely in the eye of the beholder. 

''You, possibly: "That's what aspects dictate!"''
Theoretically, one is supposed to be able to derive a sense of whether the connection is one of friction (i.e. square/opposite), harmony (i.e. trine, sextile) or neutral amplification (i.e. conjunct) based on the particular aspect between two planets/bodies.

Critically, however, as any attentive astrologer would tell you, the interpretation is not that cut and dry. The actual practical dynamic of any aspect depends on the planets/bodies in question and the natal charts involved. This isn't some cold read thing, it's basic lore. "Aspect vibe" is a rule of thumb in the sense that "I before E, except after C" applies to a fair few English words. But astrology, like English, has some pretty noteworthy grammar quirks which are predictable once you learn the rules.

This was observable when I started building the "Body Dynamics" module for EphemeralDaddy.

Secondly, to my point about the reaction of individual natal charts to any sort of dynamic: Some people thrive under pressure. Sometimes the pressure keeps the relationship fresh. Some people find too much ease/harmony stagnating. Others are easily discouraged, or already under too much pressure (overstimulated). 

It really depends on the individuals involved, and the context (for instance - if we examine this purely through an astrological lens - the personal and/or global [[transits]] also tell us something about whether or not a given dynamic might feel overpowering vs inspiring at a given moment in time).

[[🔙->Theories]]


