:: chart configurations {"position":"1225,1250","size":"100,100"}
What do I mean 'chart configurations'?

I'm using that term because one is specifically ill advised to make sweeping generalizations about "all Libra suns" or "all Aries Moons".

It does seem that there are broad generalizations that apply fairly uniquely to Libra Suns and Aries Moons, but any of those generalizations can be undermined in practice by competing elements within the chart.

Maybe someone has an Aries Moon, and the blunt, direct simplicity of his or her emotion is apparent at times, but it could well be buried under a Pisces Sun, Rising, Mars, Mercury stellium and a sprinkling of Gemini and Aquarian elements. All of the sudden, what was predicted to be a simple and direct chart just became awash in complexity, emotional nuance and devil's advocacy, especially if other planets/bodies in the chart further dilute/negate the Aries moon's impact on self expression.

That is even //more// true in cases wherein the sign or position in question is already inherently a receptive/permeable one such as Libra, Gemini or Pisces, which is renowned for some level of shapeshifting/chameleonic behavior.

Taurus appears especially prone to cultural uptake, and so if the elements in Taurus are opposed by other chart elements and his or her own native culture alike, the description of Taurus will always necessarily be relative to the rules of his or her traditions, even those which may appear at a glance to contradict certain Taurean norms.

The point is, no single aspect of an astrological chart can or should be taken in isolation. The chart must be regarded & analyzed holistically, which admittedly makes concrete analysis a lot trickier! Simply reading keywords is not going to give you a completely accurate portrait of a person, in many cases.

This may seem like a clever 'unfalsifiable claim' by astrology that prevents it from ever being thoroughly debunked, but unfortunately, it is also the only way it could possibly work in a system as complex as sentient existence. :-\

(Don't think I don't know how annoying that is. I'm still attempting to understand its validity, regardless.)

